WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.420
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.356
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.356 --> 00:00:05.472
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:06.930
CLAIRE HALLORAN:
Today, we're going

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.740
to be doing goody bag
1, atoms and reactions.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.530
Our objectives today
are to identify material

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.860
based on its properties, observe
a simple chemical reaction,

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:19.140
and identify the
limiting reagent,

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.140
and estimate the number
of atoms in a real object.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:25.830
To do this goody bag, you'll
need vinegar, a plastic cup,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:29.400
a ruler, metal samples of
aluminum, copper, iron,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.560
magnesium, and tin,
but if you can only

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.640
get copper and magnesium,
it will work, and a pipette.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.440
Some conceptual
questions to think about

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.510
while you're doing
this goody bag are,

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.520
how can we tell
different metals apart,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:45.390
and how can we count atoms
that are too small to see?

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.220
First, we have five
samples of metal

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.400
that we would like to identify
using their properties.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.330
The easiest to identify is
copper by its orange-ish color.

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:55.830
Next, we're going
to try to find out

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:59.970
which sample is iron by testing
how easy they are to bend.

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:02.320
Iron will be the most
difficult to bend.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.470
So this sample bends very
easily just with my fingers,

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.410
so it must not be iron.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:09.360
This sample is very
difficult. I can hardly

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.740
get it to bend at
all with my fingers,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:14.220
and this sample also
bends fairly easily just

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.480
using my fingers.

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:19.210
So we know that this sample, the
hardest to bend, must be iron.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.810
Next, we're going to try
to compare the density

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.370
of these different metals.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.550
So this metal feels the
heaviest and most dense.

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.590
This one's feels
lighter, and this sample

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.010
feels light as well.

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:32.370
So we know that tin is
the most dense metal,

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:34.380
so this sample must be tin.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.840
To identify which of
these samples is magnesium

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.543
and which of them
is aluminum, we're

00:01:38.543 --> 00:01:40.710
going to test which one of
them reacts with vinegar.

00:01:49.425 --> 00:01:51.300
So we're going to place
each of these samples

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.340
on the lid of our
plastic cup to make

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.100
sure we don't spill
any of the vinegar

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.470
and use our pipette to
put a drop of vinegar

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.750
on each of the samples,
and the one that reacts

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.510
will be magnesium, because
we know magnesium reacts

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.145
with vinegar watch closely.

00:02:11.072 --> 00:02:13.030
It doesn't look like
we're getting any reaction

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:16.428
from this metal, so it
must be the aluminum

00:02:16.428 --> 00:02:17.470
now, let's try the other.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.420
You can see that this material's
forming tiny bubbles after we

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:29.300
put the vinegar on it,
so it must be magnesium.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:31.160
Those bubbles are hydrogen gas.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.250
Now, that the bubbling has
stopped on our magnesium,

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.290
we note that the
reaction is over.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.220
There's still plenty
of magnesium left,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.030
so this indicates that
the acetic acid inside

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.810
of the vinegar must be
the limiting reagent.

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:51.360
Finally, we want to
estimate how many atoms

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:52.680
are in our sample of copper.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:59.200
To do so, we're
going to use a ruler.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.380
Using our ruler, we can measure
the width and the height

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.410
of this copper sample.

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:06.640
So using the metric
side of our ruler,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.700
we align the 0 with the
edge of our material

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:14.980
and see that it is about
13 millimeter wide and 25

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:16.750
millimeter long.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.730
Today, we did goody bag 1.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:22.810
We identified our metal
samples based on their material

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.600
properties, we observed
a simple reaction

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.810
and identified the
limiting reagent,

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.850
and we measured our copper to
estimate the number of atoms

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:32.700
in our sample.

